{
  "mn16:0.1": "Middle Discourses 16 ",
  "mn16:0.2": "Hard-heartedness ",
  "mn16:1.1": "So I have heard. ",
  "mn16:1.2": "At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. ",
  "mn16:1.3": "There the Buddha addressed the mendicants, ",
  "mn16:1.4": "“Mendicants!” ",
  "mn16:1.5": "“Venerable sir,” they replied. ",
  "mn16:1.6": "The Buddha said this: ",
  "mn16:2.1": "“Mendicants, when a mendicant has not given up five kinds of hard-heartedness and severed five shackles of the heart, it’s not possible for them to achieve growth, improvement, or maturity in this teaching and training. ",
  "mn16:3.1": "What are the five kinds of hard-heartedness they haven’t given up? ",
  "mn16:3.2": "Firstly, a mendicant has doubts about the Teacher. They’re uncertain, undecided, and lacking confidence. ",
  "mn16:3.3": "This being so, their mind doesn’t incline toward keenness, commitment, persistence, and striving. ",
  "mn16:3.4": "This is the first kind of hard-heartedness they haven’t given up. ",
  "mn16:4.1": "Furthermore, a mendicant has doubts about the teaching … ",
  "mn16:4.2": "This is the second kind of hard-heartedness. ",
  "mn16:5.1": "They have doubts about the Saṅgha … ",
  "mn16:5.2": "This is the third kind of hard-heartedness. ",
  "mn16:6.1": "They have doubts about the training … ",
  "mn16:6.2": "",
  "mn16:6.3": "This is the fourth kind of hard-heartedness. ",
  "mn16:7.1": "Furthermore, a mendicant is angry and ups